### Why can a sealed type not be local or anonymous?

A sealed type uses the form `public sealed interface AccountProduct permits SavingsProduct, CurrentProduct, FixedDepositProduct`. The `permits` clause lists the exact subtypes allowed. The compiler needs every permitted subtype to be a top-level or nested named type that it can resolve, because it writes the permitted list into the class file as the `PermittedSubclasses` attribute.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Valid: top-level sealed interface with named subtypes
public sealed interface AccountProduct
        permits SavingsProduct, CurrentProduct, FixedDepositProduct {
    BigDecimal monthlyCharge();
}

final class SavingsProduct implements AccountProduct {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyCharge() {
        return new BigDecimal("150.00");
    }
}

final class CurrentProduct implements AccountProduct {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyCharge() {
        return new BigDecimal("300.00");
    }
}

final class FixedDepositProduct implements AccountProduct {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyCharge() {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    }
}
```

### What happens if you try to seal a local class?

A local class exists only inside a method body. The compiler rejects the `sealed` modifier on it because the class cannot be referenced from a `permits` clause outside that method.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class InvalidLocalSealed {
    public void process() {
        // COMPILE ERROR: sealed not allowed on local class
        // sealed class Temp { }

        // This does not compile because:
        // 1. Temp's name is scoped to this method
        // 2. No permits clause can reference it externally
        // 3. The PermittedSubclasses attribute needs resolvable names
    }
}
```

### What happens if you try to seal an anonymous class?

An anonymous class has no name at all. Since sealing is about naming a closed set of heirs, `sealed` is meaningless and disallowed on anonymous classes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InvalidAnonymousSealed {
    interface AccountProduct {
        BigDecimal monthlyCharge();
    }

    public void process() {
        // COMPILE ERROR: cannot apply sealed to anonymous class
        // AccountProduct p = new sealed AccountProduct() {
        //     public BigDecimal monthlyCharge() {
        //         return new BigDecimal("200.00");
        //     }
        // };
    }
}
```

### Can a lambda implement a sealed interface?

No. A lambda cannot implement a sealed interface because the compiler cannot synthesize a named permitted subtype for the lambda's generated class. A functional interface used by a lambda must be effectively open for the compiler to create the implementation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LambdaSealedDemo {
    // This sealed functional interface cannot be used with a lambda
    sealed interface FeeCalculator permits StandardFee {
        BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal);
    }

    final static class StandardFee implements FeeCalculator {
        @Override
        public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal) {
            return principal.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This DOES work: using the named permitted subclass
        FeeCalculator calc = new StandardFee();
        System.out.println(calc.calculate(new BigDecimal("100000")));

        // This does NOT compile:
        // FeeCalculator lambdaCalc = (p) -> p.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
        // Lambda target type must not be a sealed interface
    }
}
```

```text
2000.00
```

### How does the PermittedSubclasses attribute work?

The compiler records the permitted subtypes in a class-file attribute called `PermittedSubclasses`, listing them by fully qualified binary name. The JVM re-validates this at load time. Local and anonymous classes lack stable binary names that can be written into this attribute.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PermittedSubclassReflection {
    sealed interface AccountProduct
            permits SavingsProduct, CurrentProduct {}
    final record SavingsProduct(String name) implements AccountProduct {}
    final record CurrentProduct(String name) implements AccountProduct {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Reflection shows the permitted subtypes
        Class<?>[] permitted = AccountProduct.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        System.out.println("isSealed: " + AccountProduct.class.isSealed());
        Arrays.stream(permitted)
                .forEach(c -> System.out.println("Permitted: " + c.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

```text
isSealed: true
Permitted: SavingsProduct
Permitted: CurrentProduct
```

### Can a local or anonymous class extend a sealed type?

A local or anonymous class can extend or implement a sealed type only through a `non-sealed` branch. It cannot be a direct permitted subtype (those must be named), but it can extend a `non-sealed` permitted subtype, which reopens the hierarchy.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LocalExtendingNonSealed {

    sealed interface AccountProduct
            permits StandardProduct, OpenProduct {}

    final record StandardProduct(BigDecimal fee) implements AccountProduct {}
    non-sealed interface OpenProduct extends AccountProduct {}

    public void process() {
        // Local class extending the non-sealed branch: allowed
        class PromoProduct implements OpenProduct {
            public BigDecimal discount() {
                return new BigDecimal("50.00");
            }
        }

        PromoProduct promo = new PromoProduct();
        System.out.println("Promo discount: " + promo.discount() + " INR");

        // Anonymous class extending non-sealed: also allowed
        OpenProduct anonProduct = new OpenProduct() {};
        System.out.println("Anonymous product created");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new LocalExtendingNonSealed().process();
    }
}
```

```text
Promo discount: 50.00 INR
Anonymous product created
```

### What are the design implications?

The restriction is actually the feature working correctly. If the language allowed sealing a local or anonymous class, the closed-set guarantee would be meaningless because those types cannot be named on a permits list. The restriction forces proper architecture: sealed hierarchies use named, top-level or nested types that auditors and the compiler can enumerate.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Production pattern: sealed hierarchy with named types
public sealed interface PaymentEvent
        permits PaymentEvent.Transfer, PaymentEvent.Deposit, PaymentEvent.Withdrawal {

    record Transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentEvent {}
    record Deposit(String account, BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentEvent {}
    record Withdrawal(String account, BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentEvent {}
}
```

Pankaj, the architect, designs all sealed hierarchies at National Bank of India using named records as leaves, which gives both the closed-set guarantee and clean pattern-matching support.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The key senior point is that sealing exists to provide a stable, provable closed hierarchy for exhaustive switch and compile-time verification. Local and anonymous types are ephemeral and scoped, so they cannot provide that guarantee. This is why sealed hierarchies should always be modeled with named types at the top or nested level, using records where possible for clean deconstruction in pattern matching.
